# ==========================================================================
# Managed by CanonSync — canonical .gitattributes (block mode)
# Source of truth: canon/gitattributes.master · Do not hand-edit inside a synced repo.
# ==========================================================================

# ==========================================================================
# Canonical .gitattributes  --  SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This is the one file to edit. CanonSync copies the block below into a
# managed region inside every repo's .gitattributes.
#
# Do NOT hand-edit the managed block inside an individual repo -- your edit
# will be overwritten on the next sync. Edit THIS file, then run the sync.
# Anything a repo adds OUTSIDE its managed block is preserved untouched.
#
# Add your own project-specific rules in each repo BELOW the managed
# block (under the "repo-specific attributes" separator), not here.
# This includes things like Git LFS tracking for large binaries, which
# only some repos need.
#
# This template is intentionally generic (no project/ecosystem-specific
# names) so it can be dropped into ANY repo regardless of stack. Sections
# are labeled by purpose so it's easy to scan, trim, or extend. Fully
# opt-in / commented sections are for behaviors not every repo wants
# (Git LFS, linguist overrides, export-ignore, union-merge changelogs,
# etc.) -- uncomment per-repo below the separator, or uncomment here in
# the master if it should apply to every repo going forward.
# ==========================================================================

# --- Line ending normalization ---
# Let Git decide text vs. binary automatically, and normalize all detected
# text files to LF in the repository (checked out as LF everywhere unless
# a more specific rule below overrides it, e.g. for Windows batch/PowerShell).
* text=auto eol=lf

# --- Explicit text types: source code ---
*.py    text
*.pyi   text
*.pyw   text
*.ipynb text
*.js    text
*.mjs   text
*.cjs   text
*.jsx   text
*.ts    text
*.tsx   text
*.java  text
*.kt    text
*.kts   text
*.c     text
*.h     text
*.cpp   text
*.hpp   text
*.cs    text
*.go    text
*.rs    text
*.rb    text
*.php   text
*.swift text
*.r     text
*.m     text
*.mm    text
*.pl    text
*.lua   text
*.sql   text

# --- Explicit text types: shell / scripts ---
# Unix shells and Makefiles must always stay LF, even checked out on
# Windows, or the shebang / interpreter parsing breaks.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.bash  text eol=lf
*.zsh   text eol=lf
Makefile text eol=lf
makefile text eol=lf

# Windows-native scripts must always stay CRLF, or cmd.exe / PowerShell
# can misbehave on some versions when handed a bare-LF script.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# --- Explicit text types: docs / config / data ---
*.md    text
*.rst   text
*.txt   text
*.tex   text
*.json  text
*.jsonc text
*.yml   text
*.yaml  text
*.toml  text
*.cfg   text
*.ini   text
*.conf  text
*.xml   text
*.csv   text
*.tsv   text
*.svg   text
*.env.example text

# --- Explicit text types: web ---
*.html  text
*.htm   text
*.css   text
*.scss  text
*.sass  text
*.less  text

# --- Explicit binary types: images ---
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.bmp   binary
*.tiff  binary

# --- Explicit binary types: documents / archives ---
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.7z    binary
*.rar   binary
*.tar   binary
*.gz    binary

# --- Explicit binary types: compiled / executable ---
*.exe   binary
*.dll   binary
*.so    binary
*.dylib binary
*.class binary
*.pyc   binary
*.pyd   binary
*.whl   binary

# --- Explicit binary types: fonts / media ---
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.eot   binary
*.mp3   binary
*.mp4   binary
*.mov   binary
*.wav   binary

# --- Explicit binary types: databases ---
*.sqlite binary
*.sqlite3 binary
*.db    binary

# --- Diff readability for common lockfiles / generated data files ---
# These are still text (so line-ending rules apply), but a raw line-by-line
# diff is rarely useful for a machine-generated lockfile or notebook.
# Uncomment per-repo (or here for all repos) if noisy diffs on these
# specific files become a recurring annoyance.
# package-lock.json -diff
# poetry.lock       -diff
# Pipfile.lock      -diff
# *.ipynb           -diff

# --- Merge strategy overrides ---
# "union" merge means both sides' additions are kept on conflict instead
# of forcing a manual resolution -- useful for append-only files like
# changelogs where two branches both added new entries.
# Uncomment per-repo for files that fit this pattern.
# CHANGELOG.md merge=union

# --- GitHub Linguist overrides (language stats / diff visibility) ---
# Use these to keep a repo's "Languages" bar and diff view accurate when
# generated code, vendored dependencies, or non-primary files would
# otherwise skew or clutter it. All commented by default -- uncomment
# and adjust paths per-repo as needed.
# vendor/**      linguist-vendored
# third_party/** linguist-vendored
# dist/**        linguist-generated
# build/**       linguist-generated
# *.min.js       linguist-generated
# *.lock         linguist-generated
# docs/**        linguist-documentation

# --- git archive / export-ignore ---
# Files matched here are excluded from `git archive` output (e.g. release
# tarballs/zipballs), even though they remain tracked in the repo itself.
# Useful for CI configs, test fixtures, or internal tooling that shouldn't
# ship in a source distribution. Uncomment and adjust per-repo.
# .github/       export-ignore
# tests/         export-ignore
# .gitattributes export-ignore
# .gitignore     export-ignore

# --- Git LFS (opt-in, uncomment per repo that ships large binary assets) ---
# *.zip  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# *.pdf  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# *.psd  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# *.mp4  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# *.iso  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# ==========================================================================
# --- repo-specific attributes (not managed) ---
# Anything a repo needs beyond the shared block goes below this line --
# CanonSync will never touch it.
# ==========================================================================
